# 2. Problem Statement

Customers in Bauchi often need products from nearby local shops but may not be able to travel to purchase them. At the same time, many local businesses have products and customers but lack a structured digital ordering and delivery channel.

Independent motorcycle and Keke NAPEP operators also have available delivery capacity that is not consistently connected to customer demand.

Existing informal delivery arrangements can provide limited visibility, verification, accountability, and operational data. Customers may be uncertain about:

* Whether a product is actually available.
* Which business will fulfill an order.
* Whether the complete order can be supplied.
* How much delivery will cost.
* Whether an order has been picked up.
* Where the order is within the delivery process.
* Whether the delivery has been successfully completed.

SQUADLINK addresses this gap by creating a structured digital coordination layer between customers, businesses, and independent riders.

## Problem Evidence / Assumptions to Validate

The MVP is based on the following assumptions:

1. Customers need a simple way to browse a local catalogue and place a trackable delivery order using a phone or computer.
2. An order should only be confirmed once a single business has been verified as capable of supplying the complete order.
3. Pickup and delivery verification using a pickup code and delivery OTP can materially improve trust and accountability compared with informal arrangements.
4. Independent motorcycle and Keke NAPEP operators represent existing delivery capacity that can be coordinated rather than newly created.

These assumptions should be validated further through informal interviews, observation of existing local delivery arrangements, and pilot data when available.

